1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,632
This is STAT 331, video 13 b. Video 13 a wrote the model y equals X beta plus epsilon, and interpreted the coefficients. Now we estimate beta.

2
00:00:14,082 --> 00:00:20,546
This video prepares you for three question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:20,996 --> 00:00:29,017
Type C: derive the least squares estimate, beta hat equals X transpose X inverse, X transpose y.

4
00:00:29,467 --> 00:00:34,438
Type D: show that the maximum likelihood estimate of beta is the same.

5
00:00:34,888 --> 00:00:47,197
Type E: compute beta hat with the matrix formula, as in Tutorial 2, question 3, and practice question 1 of Lecture 6. Then we interpret the result with card B.

6
00:00:47,647 --> 00:01:06,677
Section 1. We do not know beta. We estimate it with least squares. For any candidate beta, y minus X beta is the vector of differences between the data and the candidate means. S of beta is this vector times itself. That is the sum of the squared differences.

7
00:01:07,127 --> 00:01:22,316
Here is a picture with our own small data set, with one covariate. Take the candidate beta 0 equals 3 and beta 1 equals 0.3. Each orange square has the area of one squared difference.

8
00:01:22,766 --> 00:01:34,051
S is a function of beta. On the right, each gray curve joins the values of beta with the same S. The candidate gives S equal to 5.55.

9
00:01:34,501 --> 00:01:50,736
The least squares estimate beta hat is the value of beta with the smallest S. Here it is 1.3 and 0.9, with S equal to 1.9. The squares are smaller. Next, we find beta hat with calculus.

10
00:01:51,186 --> 00:01:59,122
Section 2. Question type C asks you to derive the least squares estimate. Method card C has five steps.

11
00:01:59,572 --> 00:02:10,644
Step 1: write S of beta, y minus X beta transpose, times y minus X beta. The picture shows S along one coordinate of beta.

12
00:02:11,094 --> 00:02:16,662
Step 2: expand S. Two of the four terms are the same number. Combine them.

13
00:02:17,112 --> 00:02:29,677
Step 3: take the derivative with respect to the vector beta, one term at a time, with the two rules of Lecture 5. The orange tangent shows a derivative that is not 0.

14
00:02:30,127 --> 00:02:40,901
Step 4: set the derivative to the zero vector, and put a hat on beta. At the lowest point of S, the tangent is flat. This gives the normal equations.

15
00:02:41,351 --> 00:02:47,879
Step 5: solve for beta hat. This needs the columns of X to be linearly independent.

16
00:02:48,329 --> 00:03:04,137
Step 1 of card C: write S of beta. Pause the video and try it. y minus X beta is a vector with n entries. S is this vector transposed, times itself. So S is the sum of the squared entries, one number.

17
00:03:04,587 --> 00:03:18,752
The transpose of y minus X beta is y transpose, minus beta transpose X transpose, in orange. The reason: the transpose of a product A B is B transpose A transpose.

18
00:03:19,202 --> 00:03:29,549
Step 2: multiply the two brackets. Each part of the first bracket multiplies each part of the second. This gives four terms. The grid shows them.

19
00:03:29,999 --> 00:03:47,343
Look at the term y transpose X beta. Its shape is 1 by n, times n by p plus 1, times p plus 1 by 1. So it is 1 by 1: one number. A number equals its transpose. So the two yellow terms are equal.

20
00:03:47,793 --> 00:04:01,275
Add the two middle terms. S of beta equals y transpose y, minus 2 y transpose X beta, plus beta transpose X transpose X beta. This is slide 19.

21
00:04:01,725 --> 00:04:13,373
Step 3 of card C: take the derivative of S with respect to the vector beta, one term at a time. Line 5 is the expanded S. Pause the video and try it.

22
00:04:13,823 --> 00:04:20,245
The first term, y transpose y, has no beta in it. Its derivative is the zero vector.

23
00:04:20,695 --> 00:04:35,073
The second term, minus 2 y transpose X beta, is a transpose beta, with a equal to minus 2 X transpose y. By rule 1, its derivative is a: minus 2 X transpose y.

24
00:04:35,523 --> 00:04:51,886
The third term is a quadratic form in beta, with the matrix X transpose X. This matrix is symmetric, because its transpose is X transpose X again. By rule 2, the derivative is 2 X transpose X beta.

25
00:04:52,336 --> 00:05:02,853
Step 4: add the three parts, set the sum equal to the zero vector, and put a hat on beta. In the picture, the tangent is flat at the lowest point.

26
00:05:03,303 --> 00:05:19,175
Add 2 X transpose y to both sides and divide by 2. X transpose X, times beta hat, equals X transpose y. These are the normal equations: p plus 1 equations, one for each coefficient.

27
00:05:19,625 --> 00:05:34,324
Step 5: multiply both sides on the left by the inverse of X transpose X. Beta hat equals X transpose X inverse, X transpose y. In the picture, beta hat is the lowest point.

28
00:05:34,774 --> 00:05:42,262
This is the least squares estimate for every linear model in this course. Question type E uses it in step 5.

29
00:05:42,712 --> 00:05:59,160
Step 5 needs the inverse of X transpose X. The slide adds a condition: the columns of X must be linearly independent. Here is an example where they are not. Suppose X has age in years and also age in months.

30
00:05:59,610 --> 00:06:20,047
Column 3 is 12 times column 2. Then X transpose X has no inverse. Many different beta give the same X beta, for example beta 1 equals 12 and beta 2 equals 0, or beta 1 equals 0 and beta 2 equals 1. The data cannot choose between them.

31
00:06:20,497 --> 00:06:29,777
If no column is a linear combination of the other columns, X transpose X is invertible, and there is exactly one beta hat.

32
00:06:30,227 --> 00:06:47,614
The normal equations also give a check. Start from X transpose X beta hat equals X transpose y. In the picture, y is one vector with n entries. All vectors X beta form a flat space, the column space of X, in purple.

33
00:06:48,064 --> 00:07:00,139
Subtract X transpose X beta hat from both sides, and take out X transpose. In the picture, X beta hat is y hat, the fitted vector. It is in the column space.

34
00:07:00,589 --> 00:07:09,037
y minus X beta hat is the residual vector e, in orange. So X transpose e equals the zero vector.

35
00:07:09,487 --> 00:07:24,868
Row j of X transpose is column j of X. So every column of X times e is 0: e is perpendicular to every column. So y hat is the point of the column space that is closest to y.

36
00:07:25,318 --> 00:07:32,059
When the model has an intercept, column 1 of X is all ones. So the residuals add to 0.

37
00:07:32,509 --> 00:07:48,744
For our own data, the residuals are minus 0.2, minus 0.1, 1, minus 0.9, and 0.2. Their sum is 0. Card E uses X transpose e equals 0 as its check.

38
00:07:49,194 --> 00:08:02,079
Section 3. There is a second way to estimate beta: maximum likelihood. Question type D asks you to show that it gives the same beta hat. Method card D has five steps.

39
00:08:02,529 --> 00:08:14,689
Step 1: write the likelihood. Under our assumptions, y is M V N with mean X beta and variance sigma squared I. The likelihood is this M V N density.

40
00:08:15,139 --> 00:08:20,046
Step 2: simplify the determinant and the inverse of sigma squared I.

41
00:08:20,496 --> 00:08:26,341
Step 3: take the log. This gives the log-likelihood, l of beta and sigma squared.

42
00:08:26,791 --> 00:08:40,999
Step 4: find the terms that contain beta. They are minus S of beta over 2 sigma squared. The blue curve is the log-likelihood. It is the orange curve S, turned upside down and moved.

43
00:08:41,449 --> 00:08:51,113
Step 5: so the beta with the largest log-likelihood is the beta with the smallest S. The M L E of beta is beta hat.

44
00:08:51,563 --> 00:09:04,363
Card D on slides 21 and 22. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: the model says y is M V N, with mean X beta and variance sigma squared I.

45
00:09:04,813 --> 00:09:12,323
The likelihood is the M V N density, with mu equal to X beta and Sigma equal to sigma squared I.

46
00:09:12,773 --> 00:09:31,695
Step 2: simplify the two marked parts. sigma squared I is diagonal. The determinant of a diagonal matrix is the product of the diagonal: sigma squared to the power n. The inverse inverts each diagonal entry: 1 over sigma squared, times I.

47
00:09:32,145 --> 00:09:51,388
Step 3: take the log. The first term is minus n over 2 log 2 pi. The determinant gives minus n over 2 log sigma squared. The exponent gives minus 1 over 2 sigma squared, times y minus X beta transpose, y minus X beta.

48
00:09:51,838 --> 00:10:06,003
Step 4: the marked product is S of beta. The first two terms do not contain beta. So l equals a term without beta, minus S of beta over 2 sigma squared. The blue curve is l.

49
00:10:06,453 --> 00:10:19,531
Step 5: 1 over 2 sigma squared is positive. So the largest l comes from the smallest S, for every sigma squared. The M L E of beta is the least squares estimate.

50
00:10:19,981 --> 00:10:33,975
M L E equals O L S, when all our assumptions hold. The M L E uses the normal errors. Least squares only minimizes the sum of squares, and it does not need normal errors.

51
00:10:34,425 --> 00:10:43,129
Section 4. Question type E asks you to compute beta hat with the matrix formula. Method card E has six steps.

52
00:10:43,579 --> 00:10:46,353
Step 1: write X and y, with card A.

53
00:10:46,803 --> 00:10:57,384
Step 2: X transpose X. Its entry j, k is column j of X times column k of X: multiply the entries row by row, and add.

54
00:10:57,834 --> 00:11:09,738
Step 3: invert X transpose X. For a 2 by 2 matrix, divide by a d minus b c, swap the diagonal, and change the sign of the other two entries.

55
00:11:10,188 --> 00:11:16,225
Step 4: X transpose y. Its entry j is column j of X times y.

56
00:11:16,675 --> 00:11:22,521
Step 5: beta hat is X transpose X inverse, times X transpose y.

57
00:11:22,971 --> 00:11:29,947
Step 6: check that X transpose e is the zero vector, where e is y minus X beta hat.

58
00:11:30,397 --> 00:11:48,125
Now a tutorial question of type E. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 2, question 3 c: calculate X transpose X inverse. Step 1 of card E: X and y come from part b. X has the columns P 1 and P 2.

59
00:11:48,575 --> 00:12:01,844
Step 2: entry 1 1 of X transpose X is column 1 times column 1. Multiply row by row and add: 1 plus 1 plus 0 plus 1 plus 0, which is 3.

60
00:12:02,294 --> 00:12:15,926
Entry 1 2 is column 1 times column 2. Only row 1 has a 1 in both columns. So the entry is 1. Entry 2 1 is the same, because X transpose X is symmetric.

61
00:12:16,376 --> 00:12:28,536
Entry 2 2 is column 2 times column 2: 1 plus 0 plus 1 plus 0 plus 1, which is 3. So X transpose X is 3, 1, 1, 3.

62
00:12:28,986 --> 00:12:36,111
Step 3: invert it. a d minus b c is 3 times 3, minus 1 times 1, which is 8.

63
00:12:36,561 --> 00:12:59,175
Swap the diagonal, change the sign of the other entries, and divide by 8. The inverse is 0.375 and minus 0.125 in row 1, and minus 0.125 and 0.375 in row 2. This is the answer to part c, as in the posted solution.

64
00:12:59,625 --> 00:13:11,358
Part d: compute the least squares estimates. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card E is done: X and y are in the table. Steps 2 and 3 were part c.

65
00:13:11,808 --> 00:13:23,819
Step 4: X transpose y. Entry 1 is column 1 times y. The marked rows have x 1 equal to 1: 11 plus 2 plus 4, which is 17.

66
00:13:24,269 --> 00:13:33,741
Entry 2 is column 2 times y. The marked rows have x 2 equal to 1: 11 plus 8 plus 6, which is 25.

67
00:13:34,191 --> 00:13:41,039
Step 5: beta hat is X transpose X inverse, from part c, times X transpose y.

68
00:13:41,489 --> 00:13:53,350
Row 1: 3 times 17, minus 25, is 26. Row 2: minus 17, plus 3 times 25, is 58. Both are over 8.

69
00:13:53,800 --> 00:14:03,549
Divide by 8. Beta 1 hat is 3.25 and beta 2 hat is 7.25. This is the answer to part d.

70
00:14:03,999 --> 00:14:30,218
Step 6: check. The fitted values are 3.25 x 1 plus 7.25 x 2: 10.5, 3.25, 7.25, 3.25, 7.25. The residuals are y minus the fitted values: 0.5, minus 1.25, 0.75, 0.75, minus 1.25.

71
00:14:30,668 --> 00:14:51,511
Column 1 times e: 0.5 minus 1.25 plus 0.75 is 0. Column 2 times e: 0.5 plus 0.75 minus 1.25 is 0. So X transpose e is 0, and the estimates pass the check.

72
00:14:51,961 --> 00:15:01,987
Now card B again, on Tutorial 2, question 3 e. Pause the video and try it. Interpret beta 2 hat, which is 7.25.

73
00:15:02,437 --> 00:15:18,117
Step 1 of card B: write the fitted mean: 3.25 x 1 plus 7.25 x 2. The bars show it for the four settings: 0, 7.25, 3.25, and 10.5.

74
00:15:18,567 --> 00:15:32,285
Step 3: change x 2 from 0 to 1, and keep x 1 fixed. With x 1 equal to 0, the fitted mean goes from 0 to 7.25. The difference is 7.25.

75
00:15:32,735 --> 00:15:41,460
With x 1 equal to 1, it goes from 3.25 to 10.5. The difference is again 7.25.

76
00:15:41,910 --> 00:15:54,710
Step 4, the answer: when newspaper advertising is used, the estimated mean increase in market share is 7.25 higher, with the use of point of sale held fixed.

77
00:15:55,160 --> 00:16:08,472
Practice question 1 of Lecture 6 is a type E question with symbols. Pause the video and try it. With one covariate, show that the matrix formula gives the simple linear regression estimates.

78
00:16:08,922 --> 00:16:17,605
Step 1 of card E: with one covariate and an intercept, X has two columns: ones, and the x i. It is n by 2.

79
00:16:18,055 --> 00:16:31,239
The goal is beta 1 hat equals S x y over S x x, and beta 0 hat equals y bar minus beta 1 hat x bar. The plan is card E, steps 2 to 5.

80
00:16:31,689 --> 00:16:49,246
Step 2 of card E: X transpose X. X transpose is 2 by n, and X is n by 2, so X transpose X is 2 by 2. Entry 1 1 is row 1 of X transpose times column 1 of X: the sum of n ones, which is n.

81
00:16:49,696 --> 00:17:02,560
Entry 1 2 is row 1 of X transpose times column 2 of X. It is the sum of 1 times x i, which is the sum of the x i. Entry 2 1 is the same sum.

82
00:17:03,010 --> 00:17:08,791
Entry 2 2 is row 2 times column 2. It is the sum of x i squared.

83
00:17:09,241 --> 00:17:17,604
Step 4: X transpose y is 2 by 1. Its entries are the sum of the y i, and the sum of x i y i.

84
00:17:18,054 --> 00:17:24,539
X transpose X is symmetric: the two off-diagonal entries are both the sum of x i.

85
00:17:24,989 --> 00:17:45,235
Step 3 of card E: invert X transpose X. Pause the video and try it. For a 2 by 2 matrix with entries a, b, c, d, the inverse is 1 over a d minus b c, times a new matrix. In the new matrix, swap a and d, and change the sign of b and c.

86
00:17:45,685 --> 00:18:00,149
Here a is n, b and c are the sum of x i, and d is the sum of x i squared. So a d minus b c is n times the sum of x i squared, minus the square of the sum of x i.

87
00:18:00,599 --> 00:18:07,937
The sum of x i is n x bar. So its square is n times n x bar squared. Take out n.

88
00:18:08,387 --> 00:18:20,739
The bracket, the sum of x i squared minus n x bar squared, is S x x. The second identity on the right shows it. So a d minus b c is n S x x.

89
00:18:21,189 --> 00:18:37,168
Put a, b, c, d and n S x x into line 1. The inverse is 1 over n S x x, times the matrix: the sum of x i squared, minus the sum of x i, minus the sum of x i, and n.

90
00:18:37,618 --> 00:18:43,783
n S x x is positive when the x i are not all equal. Then the inverse exists.

91
00:18:44,233 --> 00:18:51,465
Step 5 of card E: multiply the inverse by X transpose y. Pause the video and try it.

92
00:18:51,915 --> 00:19:06,486
Row 2 of the inverse is marked. Row 2 gives beta 1 hat: minus the sum of x i times the sum of y i, plus n times the sum of x i y i, all over n S x x.

93
00:19:06,936 --> 00:19:15,043
Use the first identity on the right: the sum of x i is n x bar, and the sum of y i is n y bar.

94
00:19:15,493 --> 00:19:21,722
Take out n. The bracket is the sum of x i y i, minus n x bar y bar.

95
00:19:22,172 --> 00:19:34,055
By the second identity, this bracket is S x y. The n cancels. So beta 1 hat equals S x y over S x x. This is the simple regression slope.

96
00:19:34,505 --> 00:19:47,475
Row 1 gives beta 0 hat: the sum of x i squared times the sum of y i, minus the sum of x i times the sum of x i y i, over n S x x.

97
00:19:47,925 --> 00:20:07,147
Put in the identities for the two marked sums. The sum of x i squared is S x x plus n x bar squared. The sum of x i y i is S x y plus n x bar y bar. The sums of x i and y i are n x bar and n y bar.

98
00:20:07,597 --> 00:20:13,890
Multiply out. Two terms are n squared x bar squared y bar, with opposite signs.

99
00:20:14,340 --> 00:20:17,369
The two marked terms cancel. Take out n.

100
00:20:17,819 --> 00:20:31,174
Divide by n S x x. Beta 0 hat equals y bar minus x bar times S x y over S x x. That is y bar minus beta 1 hat x bar.

101
00:20:31,624 --> 00:20:40,648
Both estimates agree with simple linear regression. In the picture, the fitted line goes through the mean point, x bar, y bar.

102
00:20:41,098 --> 00:20:53,087
Go back x bar units to x equals 0. The line goes down by beta 1 hat times x bar. So the intercept is y bar minus beta 1 hat x bar.

103
00:20:53,537 --> 00:21:15,404
Check the result with our own small data set. Pause the video and try it. n is 5. The table gives the sums: the sum of x is 15, the sum of x squared is 55, the sum of y is 20, and the sum of x y is 69. These are the entries of X transpose X and X transpose y.

104
00:21:15,854 --> 00:21:28,739
a d minus b c is 5 times 55, minus 15 squared, which is 50. x bar is 3, and S x x is 10. So n S x x is also 50.

105
00:21:29,189 --> 00:21:38,000
Swap the diagonal, change the sign of the other entries, and divide by 50. Then multiply by X transpose y.

106
00:21:38,450 --> 00:21:56,370
Row 1 is 55 times 20, minus 15 times 69, which is 65. Row 2 is minus 15 times 20, plus 5 times 69, which is 45. Divide by 50. Beta hat is 1.3 and 0.9.

107
00:21:56,820 --> 00:22:18,111
The simple regression formulas give the same. S x y is 9, so beta 1 hat is 0.9. y bar is 4, so beta 0 hat is 4 minus 0.9 times 3, which is 1.3. The fitted line is y hat equals 1.3 plus 0.9 x.

108
00:22:18,561 --> 00:22:34,838
To recap. Card C derives the least squares estimate: write S, expand it, take the derivative, set it to 0 to get the normal equations, and solve. Beta hat equals X transpose X inverse, X transpose y.

109
00:22:35,288 --> 00:22:47,448
Card D shows that the M L E is the same: write the likelihood, simplify, take the log, and find the terms with beta. They are minus S over 2 sigma squared.

110
00:22:47,898 --> 00:23:04,261
Card E computes beta hat: X transpose X, its inverse, X transpose y, the product, and the check X transpose e equals 0. The next video gives the properties of beta hat, the residuals, and sigma hat squared.
